T:Cân y Cwcwallt
T:The Cuckold's Song
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=25 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by William Henry Ellis (born 1897), a lorry driver, of Gwynfryn, Mynytho, near Pwllheli, Caernarfonshire; recorded 11.9.64 (St Fagans tape 829). He had the song from another Mynytho boy when he was about 10. There was once a further verse in the district, but he could not remember it. This is the only known Welsh version of "Our Goodman" (Child ballad 274), also known in English as "Five (Seven) Nights Drunk" and "The Old Farmer and His Young Wife": the wife explains away the white horses, coach carriages and beer casks as hunting hounds, dung carts and butter pots from her mother. In full versions elsewhere the last discovery is a stranger in the bed. Compare the tune with "Ah vous dirai-je" (sung to "Baa, baa, black sheep") and with "Hafod yr Aeres" in Nicholas Bennett's Alawon fy Ngwlad (1896).
N:Above the pickup B the score also has a quaver rest with a pause, footnoted "(a) 2 & 3", for verses 2 and 3. The grace note in bar 21 is a small note on the score, for verses with an extra syllable ("dwsin o drolia", "dwsin o botia"). The second A in bar 28 is in brackets on the score.
